                <pb facs="0306" n="298" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg298"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9822: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-31T18:00:00+08:00">August 31, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 31—11:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>620. Reference my No. 548, August 24, 5 p.m. and Nanking’s No. 563, August 30, 4
                    p.m., opinions here differ as to the probable weight of the bomb, but the weight
                    of the evidence continues to point to Chinese responsibility. Reports of Navy
                    and United States Marines observers have been forwarded by mail. My British
                    colleague tells me unofficially and confidentially that the British naval
                    authorities are also of the opinion that the responsibility is with Chinese.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department and Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
